function techniqueCallout(type){
 if(type==='guitar-one')return objectiveOneCallout('Build the key-of-G foundation and connect the beat.', ['<strong>Use the anchor finger:</strong> Keep finger 3 planted through G, Cadd9, and D; release it for Em.','<strong>Skip the pick for now.</strong> Chord shapes and strumming first - pick later.','<strong>Connect the beat:</strong> Lightly rake down with the index fingernail. Count <strong>1 · 2 · 3 · 4</strong>; the strum and tapping foot go down together.']);
 if(type==='bar-one')return objectiveOneCallout('Learn the baritone ukulele map and connect the beat.', ['Build each shape with the same lead finger every time. G and Em begin with one finger; use those easy shapes to learn how the hand moves.','Strum all four strings with a relaxed index finger. Count <strong>1 · 2 · 3 · 4</strong>; the strum and tapping foot go down together.','The visual examples use a guitar, but the fret, finger, and strumming directions work the same way on baritone ukulele.']);
 if(type==='uke-one')return objectiveOneCallout('Learn the standard ukulele map and connect the beat.', ['Build each shape with the same lead finger every time. C and Am begin with one finger; use those easy shapes to learn how the hand moves.','Strum all four strings with a relaxed index finger. Count <strong>1 · 2 · 3 · 4</strong>; the strum and tapping foot go down together.','The visual examples use a guitar, but the fret, finger, and strumming directions work the same way on standard ukulele.']);
 if(type==='guitar-two')return `<aside class="strum-callout"><div class="callout-mark">↑</div><div><p>OBJECTIVE NOTES</p><h3>Move beyond one key and unlock more songs.</h3><ul><li>The key of G can only take us so far. C and Am introduce crucial shapes from C major; Am also belongs to the key of G. E gives us another common major shape and leads naturally into Am.</li><li>Use one lead finger instead of moving the whole hand at once; place that finger first, then build the rest of the chord.</li><li>Keep the downbeats on <strong>1 · 2 · 3 · 4</strong>. On the final <strong>&</strong>, lightly brush upward across the highest three or four strings with the side of your thumb.</li>${objectiveOneReturn()}</ul></div></aside>`;
 if(type==='guitar-three')return `<aside class="strum-callout"><div class="callout-mark">↕</div><div><p>OBJECTIVE NOTES</p><h3>Add frequent, playable shapes before full barre chords.</h3><ul><li>A and Dm extend the familiar open-chord vocabulary. F-Alternative comes next because it appears often and begins the barre-chord discussion without requiring a full six-string barre. B7 stays here because it is both common and playable.</li><li>These chords are sequenced by frequency, musical importance, and playability rather than by completing every key in strict order.</li><li>This objective mixes the strumming learned so far. Use four steady down-strums whenever a new pattern is not comfortable yet.</li>${objectiveOneReturn()}</ul></div></aside>`;
 if(type==='guitar-four')return `<aside class="strum-callout"><div class="callout-mark">↕</div><div><p>OBJECTIVE NOTES</p><h3>Learn the two movable major-and-minor shape families.</h3><ul><li><strong>A-string shapes:</strong> B uses the movable A-major shape and Bm uses the movable A-minor shape. Both begin at fret 2; mute low E and start the strum on A.</li><li><strong>E-string shapes:</strong> F♯ uses the movable E-major shape and F♯m uses the movable E-minor shape. Both begin at fret 2 and use all six strings.</li><li>Finger 1 acts like a movable nut. Build the shape first, then roll that finger slightly toward its thumb-side edge. Keep the thumb behind the neck and use only enough pressure for a clean sound.</li><li>These same shapes travel to other frets. Higher positions and additional chord names belong in the future chord library; this objective keeps the first barre lesson focused.</li>${objectiveOneReturn()}</ul></div></aside>`;
 if(type==='uke-young')return `<aside class="strum-callout"><div class="callout-mark">↓</div><div><p>OBJECTIVE NOTES</p><h3>Simple shapes, steady counting, and small wins.</h3><ul><li>We keep songs in C on standard ukulele and G on baritone, then add another easy chord only when a song needs it.</li><li>Success can be four steady strums while counting <strong>1 · 2 · 3 · 4</strong> and tapping a foot. Timing is the hardest part to learn; the mechanical chord shapes come with time.</li><li>Practice fret numbers, finger numbers, comfortable posture, and position. A string that does not press cleanly yet is growth—not failure.</li></ul></div></aside>`;
 if(type==='uke-general')return `<aside class="strum-callout"><div class="callout-mark">↓</div><div><p>OBJECTIVE NOTES</p><h3>Build useful chord families and steady timing.</h3><ul><li>Ukulele shapes are generally quick to add, so this path focuses on useful key families and the timing needed to play them in songs.</li><li>Build each shape with the same lead finger every time. New chords can be learned as a song calls for them.</li><li>Strum down with a relaxed index finger. Count <strong>1 · 2 · 3 · 4</strong> and let the strum and tapping foot move together.</li>${objectiveOneReturn()}</ul></div></aside>`;
 if(type==='strumming')return `<aside class="strum-callout"><div class="callout-mark">↕</div><div><p>STRUMMING NOTES</p><h3>See, hear, and feel each pattern.</h3><ul><li>Open Core 4/4, 3/4, or Sixteenth-Note Patterns, then choose an exercise. Select a chord for each measure and tap its name to see the shape.</li><li>Follow Along loops both measures continuously at your chosen tempo. Use Drums when you want a stronger beat underneath the guitar.</li><li><strong>Time signatures:</strong> 4/4 has four beats per measure; 3/4 has three and often feels like “ONE-two-three.” Eighth notes split each beat into two parts, and sixteenth notes split it into four.</li><li>A dot means your strumming hand keeps moving without touching the strings. Keep counting through the space.</li><li>In the sixteenth-note exercises, each group of four arrows fits inside one foot tap; the large number cycles through beats 1–4.</li></ul></div></aside>`;
 return `<aside class="strum-callout compact-callout"><div class="callout-mark">↕</div><div><p>HOW THESE PATTERNS WORK</p><h3>Choose a starting point or build your own.</h3><ul><li><strong>Common Progression Patterns</strong> provides ready-made four-chord loops. <strong>Custom Chord Progressions</strong> lets you build a progression of any length. <strong>Requested Patterns</strong> contains longer song sections requested by students.</li><li>Open a section, then select a numbered exercise. Read each exercise left to right and continue on the next row when its measures wrap.</li><li>Tap the beat grid in a measure to start Follow Along there. It repeats from that measure to the end; tap a chord name to see its shape.</li><li>Choose the strumming pattern and tempo that fit your goal. Four steady down-strums are always a good fallback while a chord change is still new.</li></ul></div></aside>`;
}
